<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12729"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 10:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Keeping track of serial killers is far from a science. There are figures available but what do they tell us? <BR>Take one example. BTK. The killings take place in modern times in a modern American city. Seven victims linked to most likely the same murderer. This is believed to be a fact for years until 2004 when the figure was raised to eight. In just one year that figure jumped to ten. Will it continue to climb? Very likely. <BR>Now go back in time to a crowded urban English industrial city. Jack the Ripper. In a brief late summer five murders believed to have been linked to the same murder. That figure has stood and we will never know how many more there may have been. <BR>Serial killings are in many cases unreported, unlinked and often covered up. China has only recently admitted that they have had a couple of serial killers since the 1990s. They claim it&#39;s something new there. <BR>There are and historically have been less serial killers operating in countries outside the USA but many of these fewer killers were far more prolific than most of the known USA killers. Fewer killers but more victims per killer. <BR>Third world countries barely report their serial killers. Communist countries try to pretend they don&#39;t exist and modern industrialized crime fighting societies don&#39;t really know what they are dealing with. Almost all captured serial killers have more victims than were originally thought. <BR>Available statistics show that North America has 80% of the world&#39;s known serial killers of the 20th century. <BR>The USA has 76% of the world&#39;s serial killers. <BR>The south is number one followed by the west, the midwest and the northeast. California is the top state. <BR>Europe accounts for 16% with Great Britain number one followed by Germany and France. <BR>Third world countries make up 4%. <BR>BTK represents the modern mobile serial killer. <BR>Jack the Ripper represents the crowded urban inner city serial killer. <BR>Why is the USA number one. We have both. A vastly mobile society with cars and buses and trains and planes and interstates and highways and freeways and parkways and six lane surface streets. We also have crowded inner cities and big sprawling cities. We also have lots of open spaces. We also have the ways, the means and the opportunities. <BR>Icyou says pay attention to your children and that is a large part of the problem. People don&#39;t. <BR>The three most frequent common denominators of serial killers is they come from dysfunctional families, unwed mothers and divorced parents.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12729-->
<!--Post: 12732--><!--Time: 1112940660-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12732"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 11:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know this kid, we will call him &#34;james&#34;. <BR> <BR>My friend came over the other day, and we had tea. and we talked, you know how women love to talk. Well, her son went over to James house during spring break. they went to the pond on his family&#39;s  property. the dog went with them, and he kept jumpin in the water and swimming. I dont know why the dog kept gettin in the water, its still cold here, and the water in the local ponds is very cold still. so, the dog starts actin weird on one of his trips out in the water, and started drowning, and james says &#34;oh no&#34;, but in a flat weird way, almost joking. <BR> <BR>Well, the two boys just watched the dog drown. they left it in the water, and james says to my friends son, &#34;dont tell anyone, my uncle will get very mad at me&#34; <BR> <BR>Well, this freaked me out immensely. James lives with his grandparents, and ALL he does is play video games. parents arent in the picture. <BR>My friends son kept this to himself for only one day, then finally told his mother. She told her son, &#34;call James and tell him he has to tell his grandmother what happened to the dog&#34; so he did call. next day, james calls my friends son and says he told granny what happened and granny said &#34;the dog must have had a heart attack&#34; <BR> <BR>I told my friend, she needs to call the granny and subtly mention something about the dog. She needs to find out if james really told granny. <BR> <BR>Granny is gonna answer &#34;yeah, I know the dog ran off&#34; &#40;originally told to granny by james&#41; <BR> <BR>or granny is gonna answer &#34;thats so terrible that the two boys had to see the dog drown like that&#34; <BR> <BR>Well, all I can tell you about James is that he is very shy, quiet, raised by grandparents with no rules, lives on video games. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12732-->
<!--Post: 12733--><!--Time: 1112942178-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12733"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 07, 2005 - 11:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, I can tell from the way you wrote this, Itty, that you already know then that you should be very wary of James at all times. Where he is, what he&#39;s doing.  <BR> <BR>I have a relative who is now in his 40s, and he has a similar &#39;profile.&#39; Great around family, this guy, a charmer. Innocent looking, blonde hair, blue eyes, well-built. Very energetic, charismatic.  <BR> <BR>And I am certain he&#39;s a stone-cold psychopath. For one thing, I think there&#39;s a genetic component, and since I don&#39;t think I have that particular bad gene - I got all the other ones - I can admit that I still think it runs through my family on both sides to some degree - for another, this guy used to walk up to complete strangers on the street and to impress others, start wailing on the stranger. That&#39;s what we say around here - you might call it &#39;beating the crap out of a stranger.&#39; <BR> <BR>For all I know he&#39;s outgrown that egregiously antisocial behavior, but a felon check on my home state&#39;s online service the other day &#40;general felon, not just sex offender - there&#39;s none of that in the family, thank God&#41;showed he was still on probation for something.  <BR> <BR>Then there&#39;s one of my paternal great grandfathers - many in the family are certain he murdered at least two people in his day and probably pulled off more than a couple of insurance scams. He was still considered a fairly upstanding citizen in the communities where he lived. He moved around a lot - Kentucky to Florida to Tennessee. During the 20&#39;s and then the depression. With 12 children in tow. That makes him sound psycho to me right there, but I digress...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12733-->
<!--Post: 12741--><!--Time: 1112978965-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12741"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 9:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Many of the Slavic and Russian countries are strongly patriarchial. That is how Hitler got everyone to follow along without questioning. They have very strong &#39;black and white&#39; perceptions. <BR> <BR>When I worked in probation, I realized that the problem with the probationers was they were living their lifestyle. It is what they did. Period. They were not necessarily out to break laws. They just pursued their goal. No thought to consequence, no reason to consider right from wrong, no future testing: they just did it. Water seeks its own level, as their friends were the same way. A few were harmlessly entertaining, but mostly it was a sad and &#39;ain&#39;t got no sense&#39; situation. Practice makes perfect. And a good number go on to come back again and again, and often work their way up from misdemeanor to felony with ease. Social strata only contributes in a small way, as I saw doctors and lawyers as well. Yes, genetics, the family history &#40;in all ways&#41;, and only God knows what else. <BR> <BR>Steve: my family history is riddled as well. My paternal grandfather was a bootlegger and I was told made the best whiskey in Texas. He attempted occult practices &#40;I do not think he was very successful at it&#41;, beat his wife and children, and was hunted by the vigilantes. My father always said that a person died like they lived. His father&#39;s bones were recovered in the woods, cause of death unknown. I do think that someone took him out. I shudder to think that I share bloodline and genetic material with someone like that. I just learned that my maternal grandmother was illegitimate. Well, great. Her children fought like there was no tomorrow, moved to different areas of the country, and demonstrated all the &#39;bad blood&#39; that it is possible to show. I do believe that curses come down the family line. <BR> <BR>I read that Rader is German. I am not that familiar with the teachings of the Lutheran faith. We do not know what his family line held in terms of degenerates in hiding, child rearing practices by a military father, and on and on. I am not a serial killer. I could say &#39;yet&#39;, but I do just not have the energy for it. I think that what ever it is that causes one to make the turn is so minute that we may never discover it. Some things may be glaring but are not the turning point. In the time progression of Raders pictures, you can see the change in expression and eyes. Why?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12741-->
<!--Post: 12744--><!--Time: 1112981915-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12744"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 10:38 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->ld:  Thanks for the tip. I&#39;ll read it if I’m not too busy. <BR> <BR>Jo:  WOW.  Can you recommend a bibliography? <BR> <BR>On a funky note, here&#39;s a link to a good footnote on: the summary of: an outline of: the readers digest condensed version of: the cliff-notes over: the introduction to: the chapter in Freshman Psych 101 on psycopathic personalities.  <a href="http://www.dummies.com/WileyCDA/DummiesArticle/id-1217.html" target=_top>http://www.dummies.com/WileyCDA/DummiesArticle/id- 1217.html</a>. <BR> <BR>A link to “The Psychopathy Checklist - Revised &#40;PCL-R&#41; Hare, 1991” full annotated version: <a href="http://www.swin.edu.au/victims/resources/assessment/personality/psychopathy_checklist.html" target=_top>http://www.swin.edu.au/victims/resources/assessmen t/personality/psychopathy_checklist.html</a> <BR> <BR>If Hare is correct, that psychopaths are born not made, then all the pop talk about child abuse or other inappropriate child rearing as a causative factor is out the window.  Hug your kid all you want, but you’re either hugging a psychopath or you’re not.  You can’t blame the parents, or the teachers, or television, or video games. <BR> <BR>Scary stuff. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12744-->
<!--Post: 12746--><!--Time: 1112983745-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12746"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 08, 2005 - 11:09 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;In the time progression of Raders pictures, you can see the change in expression and eyes. Why?&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I realize this is perhaps a rhetorical question, but I noted very early after his arrest how Rader&#39;s photographs are never the same - I mean, it&#39;s almost eerie, how something seems to shift in each photo that&#39;s been published. <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve noticed this phenomenon in one person I was close to at one time. She <i>&#40;we were engaged to be married at one point&#41;</i> was a wickedly intelligent eldest daughter of a Methodist preacher. She was charming when we met in a sort of retro-hippie way, sexy too. But early in our relationship I noticed this strange mutability in her photographs. One photo could show a beautiful girl with a wide smile and sexy, half-lidded eyes, another show a frumpy, sullen geek girl.  <BR> <BR>Then I saw that this was seen in her behavior as well. To borrow a term from Star Trek, M. <i>&#40;like I would use her real name!&#41;</i> phase-shifted. At church, she was the Reverend&#39;s helpful, kindhearted oldest girl, the lead soprano in the choir, a real angel. In the everyday portion of our relationship - unfortunately we were one of those couples you often encounter in college who for a time become completely subsumed in one another&#39;s personalities - she was often whiny, manipulative, self-absorbed. In the non-everyday portion, even though I was her &#34;first,&#34; she was, somehow, a masochist. That is as delicate as I can put it. To be clear, I am not a sadist or a masochist, so this was a puzzle to me, at first. <BR> <BR>None of this was, in and of itself, that odd. Then, in the third and final year of our relationship, I got sick. For one week, terribly, terribly ill. Nothing I put in my body stayed, and it didn&#39;t come out solid - again, being delicate. I lost 10 pounds, ended up going to the emergency room to get IV fluids because I was dangerously dehydrated.  <BR> <BR>Later that year my breakup with her began, in part because she wanted to add a third person to the relationship, a woman, and I wasn&#39;t all about that, in spite of what you might think about the drives and desires of a typical 22-year-old guy. <i>&#40;I&#39;m 37 now, for perspective&#41;</i>.  <BR> <BR>Long story short, the night we broke up, I felt like I saw the real person for the first time - without going into detail, I gave her reason to be angry aside from just breaking up, it was true - I was culpable, for sure - but that night we were in a car parked in a parking lot near her apartment house, and she exploded. I mean <i>roared</i> at me. At some point she hit me, I&#39;m pretty sure. She was only 5&#39;0&#34; tall, but honestly, I was frightened. <i>&#40;And I&#39;m 6&#39;0&#34; and have lifted weights for most of my adult life.&#41;</i>  <BR> <BR>After the breakup I began to examine the relationship, and I began to realize something about her. She was a sociopath. Not a potential killer, necessarily - her upbringing may have saved society that burden, as she had a sister who turned out to be a very solid, upstanding citizen, a Democratic political activist in her hometown - but I began to recall jokes I&#39;d made all along in a different light. Like when I called M. &#34;my little con artist&#34; because of her ability to very subtly get people to do what she wanted - <i>every time.</i> <BR> <BR>And I thought about that time I got sick. How it wasn&#39;t too long after that she seemed to grow more and more restless in the relationship, leading up to that thing that caused it to end. Yeah, I wondered. She was already pretty computer-savvy - far more than me, with my rockin&#39; n&#39; rollin&#39; ADHD, toodling around on computers in the late 80&#39;s was just about unthinkable. She was one of the first people I ever knew who had AOL, in the early 90&#39;s. I guess I wondered if she&#39;d done any research on poisons.  <BR> <BR>In retrospect, I <b>have</b> concluded that worry about being poisoned was pure paranoia on my part. But it&#39;s telling. No other woman I&#39;ve had a serious relationship with ever gave me reason to truly suspect something that awful. Outwardly, M. didn&#39;t either. But subconsciously, reasons for worry were settling in my mind for the duration of the relationship. All of this gelled for me the day I decided to get rid of all my &#34;memorabilia&#34; from that relationship. I started going through our photographs, the ones she&#39;d given me and the ones we&#39;d taken. She changed, in every single photo. Shifted. Something was always <i>moving</i> under the surface.  <BR> <BR>What does this mean, really? I don&#39;t know. It errs towards the mystical, I admit. But she was a lesson for me in the manifold ways a kind of bland evil can be manifested in a person. She was no serial killer, for sure. I think for a time, in her way, she was sure she loved me. But there was something inside her that was never <i>moored</i>.  <BR> <BR>Who is to say that if she&#39;d been the eldest <b>son</b> of that preacher, if her sexuality was found by her at that point to bound up with violence in some form, that she wouldn&#39;t have, as that postulated male child, been a far, far more dangerous person to be close to? I think the possibility is strong. <BR> <BR>So that strange mutability I see in Rader&#39;s bland gaze from every photo I&#39;ve seen is familiar to me, and telling. Some people are simply missing something. Or they have it, but it is so weak that it never becomes rooted, anchored in their beings.  <BR> <BR>They are the Hollow Men, and Hollow Women. I find answers in poetry - my first published words were poems, so I guess that&#39;s inevitable. In T.S. Eliot&#39;s work I have found a great deal of truth, perhaps truth he didn&#39;t intend. His poem &#34;The Hollow Men&#34; - I recommend it to you strongly for a kind of abstracted insight into what I&#39;m saying. Rader was one of those men, his voice whispering across Wichita like wind in dry grass.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
